@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Randomisation)

Each student can get a different paper. The seed decides the values, so a paper can always be printed again exactly.

@ Four small bank questions that the @selectquestion examples below draw from.
Easy: 2+3 = @q(fn: 5, id=q_easy)

Harder: 12*12 = @q(fn: 144, id=q_hard)

Write the code: @q(essay, id=write_code)

Mixed up: @q(order, open the file, read a line, close the file, id=mixed_up)

@section(Random values)

@subsection(@@rand)

A random value, fixed per student seed: a range, a step grid, or a choice list.

@rand(n=18,22)

@rand(x=1,10)

@rand(2:8:2)

@rand(red, green, blue)

@rand(a=1,9) … @q(fn: @exp(a*2))
@end

@subsection(@@randsym)

Pick one symbol/word and reuse it consistently across the document.

@randsym(sym=V,I,R)

@subsection(@@formularand)

A graded formula question over randomized inputs.

@formularand(R=1,10; V=5,20; ans=V/R)

@formularand(P = V * I, V=5:12, I=1:3, solve=P, show=V I, tol=2, w=120)

@subsection(@@formulaq)

A graded formula question: the expected answer is computed from the variables, so every variant is marked correctly.

@formulaq(V/R, unit=A)

@formulaq(V = I * R, I=1:5, R=10:100, solve=V, show=I R, tol=1, w=120)

@section(Pools and shuffling)

@subsection(@@pool)

Declare a named question bank (it renders nothing on its own).

@pool(kinematics)
@item …
@endpool

@subsection(@@pick)

Pick N items at random from this inline pool.

@pick(2)
@item Q1
@item Q2
@item Q3
@endpick

@subsection(@@frompool)

Draw N random items from a named pool.

@frompool(kinematics, 3)

@subsection(@@shuffle)

Randomize the order of the item chunks inside, per student seed.

@shuffle
@item A
@item B
@endshuffle

@subsection(@@selectquestion)

Draw one question from the document's bank by id.

@selectquestion(id="s1", fromid="q_easy,q_hard")

@selectquestion(id="s2", fromid="write_code,mixed_up", toggle="lock")

@selectquestion(id="s3", fromid="q_easy", points=2)

@subsection(@@repeat)

Repeat a block N times, drawing fresh rand values each copy.

@repeat(5)

@@repl and @@rept are the same as @@repeat:
@repl(2)
A copy.
@end
@rept(2)
Another copy.
@end
